<h2>What is a normal cortisol level?</h2>
<p><strong>A cortisol result only makes sense with its sample type, collection time, unit and the lab's own reference range.</strong></p>
<p>A general chart online can't sort every person's result. Cortisol changes through the day. And a blood (serum) result can't be compared directly with a saliva or urine result.</p>

<h2 id="cortisol-levels-chart">Cortisol levels chart: blood-test reference ranges</h2>
<p><strong>These are examples from named sources, not cut-offs for diagnosis.</strong> Use the reference range on your own report.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)]">Examples of published serum cortisol reference intervals</p>
<div class="not-prose my-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-4">
    <p class="font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]"><a href="https://www.mayocliniclabs.com/test-catalog/Overview/8545" class="text-[color:var(--color-teal-dark)] hover:underline">Mayo Clinic Laboratories CORT, adults 18+</a></p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink)]"><strong>Morning (a.m.):</strong> 7–25 mcg/dL</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-4">
    <p class="font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Mayo CORT, adults 18+</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink)]"><strong>Afternoon (p.m.):</strong> 2–14 mcg/dL</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] p-4">
    <p class="font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]"><a href="https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/003693.htm" class="text-[color:var(--color-teal-dark)] hover:underline">MedlinePlus blood-test example</a></p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.9375rem] leading-snug text-[color:var(--color-ink)]"><strong>8 a.m.:</strong> 5–25 mcg/dL (138–690 nmol/L)</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>These examples don't match each other. That's why "normal cortisol" isn't one universal number.</p>
<p>Tests done after a suppression or stimulation medicine are read in their own way.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_presenting_new.webp">
  When you get a result, look for <strong>four things</strong> on the report: sample type, time collected, unit and reference range.
</ZenTip>

<h2>Normal cortisol levels by age</h2>
<p><strong>Mayo's CORT test lists separate ranges for children and one adult range starting at 18.</strong> It doesn't give a different target for each adult decade.</p>
<p>Don't make up a goal for your 30s, 40s or 60s by stretching a social-media chart.</p>
<p>For a child's result, use the age-specific range from the testing lab and the child's doctor.</p>

<h2>How does time of day change cortisol?</h2>
<p><strong>Blood cortisol usually follows a daily rhythm: higher in the morning and lower late in the evening.</strong></p>
<p>One sample is one snapshot, not a full day of hormone output. Write down when the sample was taken, and follow the instructions for that test.</p>

<h2 id="normal-levels-women">What are normal cortisol levels in women?</h2>
<p><strong>There is no single cortisol number that is normal for every woman.</strong> The <a href="https://www.mayocliniclabs.com/test-catalog/Overview/8545">Mayo CORT blood test</a> uses the adult morning and afternoon ranges above, not a separate female range.</p>
<p>Pregnancy, estrogen medicine, collection time and the lab's method all matter when reading a result.</p>
<p>Pregnancy and medicines containing estrogen can affect total blood cortisol and the proteins that carry it. Mayo's test documentation lists these and other cautions. An online "correction" for gender can't replace that context.</p>
<p>Tell your doctor which medicines and supplements you take. <strong>Don't stop a prescribed medicine to change a result</strong> without medical advice.</p>

<ZenPicture src="/app-ui/start/zen_explain.webp" caption="Blood, saliva and urine tests each measure cortisol differently." />

<h2>Are saliva and urine tests different?</h2>
<p><strong>Yes. They are different measurements.</strong> In a medical check-up, the question decides the test.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/endocrine-diseases/cushings-syndrome">NIDDK describes late-night saliva cortisol, 24-hour urine free cortisol and dexamethasone suppression testing</a>. These are some of the tests used when doctors suspect Cushing's syndrome.</p>
<p>A four-point wellness panel isn't automatically the right next step for general tiredness.</p>

